epic.intent:
Dark Mechanicus is meant to make planning human-readable and traceable, but the rules agents follow to work with it don't travel with the app. Today they live in this repository's own files (CLAUDE.md, delivery-standards, docs), written by hand after the fact. A new repository starts without them, and an old one keeps whatever version it was given.

**What it cost (2026-10-03).** A session was asked to run an epic, but it had been connected as a `planner`, which was the app's default for Claude Code. Nothing told the agent that a planner can't run epics, or that a session's role is fixed when it starts. It spent an hour asking questions and trying workarounds instead of saying "restart as orchestrator" in its first reply. The working model that would have prevented this was only in the person's head: "the session you talk to is the orchestrator, it spins up a subagent per ticket and keeps going until the checkpoint".

**Outcome wanted.** Whenever Dark Mechanicus manages a folder, it writes that folder's agent instructions and keeps them current, the same way it keeps plans current:
- the six role guides as `.claude/skills/darkmechanicus-*` skills, as **Install agent skills** already does;
- a Dark Mechanicus block in `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` with the working model. Claude Code reads CLAUDE.md. Codex, Cursor and other hosts read AGENTS.md;
- an automatic update whenever the installed app ships newer instructions, with no button to remember;
- a Git-tracked record of which instructions version the repository has and when it was synced, so changes show up in history and code review;
- a planner session says on its first call that it can't run epics, and the folder home flags a Claude Code connection that can't run them.

**Out of scope:** committing anything (the person commits), editing text outside Dark Mechanicus's block, cleaning up removed or renamed skills, and per-host files beyond CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and `.claude/skills`.
